## Caddy Is the Least Painful for Automatic HTTPS

**Caddy** is widely considered the easiest of the three because its automatic HTTPS is built-in, enabled by default, and often requires nothing more than writing a domain name in your configuration file. [1](#8c7f2e8b-abce-4b53-93eb-0eb4b653dab5)

### Why Caddy Wins

- **Zero-configuration TLS:** Once you provide a hostname, Caddy automatically obtains, installs, and renews certificates from Let's Encrypt or ZeroSSL. It also handles OCSP stapling and can transparently fall back to a secondary certificate authority if the primary is down. In many cases, you write no ACME-specific configuration at all. [2](#20816419-5169-48f1-8b54-b7d2ab6c92d1)
- **No external tooling:** Unlike nginx, Caddy does not require a separate ACME client such as Certbot. Certificate lifecycle management is handled inside the single server binary, eliminating certificate management overhead. [1](#8c7f2e8b-abce-4b53-93eb-0eb4b653dab5)
- **Security-first defaults:** HTTPS is on by default, and HTTP/3 over QUIC is enabled automatically once TLS is active. [1](#8c7f2e8b-abce-4b53-93eb-0eb4b653dab5)

### How Traefik Compares

Traefik supports automatic HTTPS natively, but it is more involved. It demands explicit configuration of certificate resolvers, challenge types, and domain policies before it will issue or renew certificates. Traefik also asks you to learn its vocabulary—routers, services, entrypoints, providers, and middlewares—before the setup feels elegant. [1](#8c7f2e8b-abce-4b53-93eb-0eb4b653dab5)

### How nginx Compares

Nginx is the most manual option. It has no native certificate provisioning and relies on external tools like Certbot, requiring manual configuration of certificate paths and renewal schedules. This creates multiple moving parts instead of one and leaves more room for human error or expired certificates. [1](#8c7f2e8b-abce-4b53-93eb-0eb4b653dab5)

### Bottom Line

If your priority is the least painful path to automatic HTTPS, **Caddy** is the clear choice: it treats secure TLS as a default behavior rather than an add-on. Traefik provides automation but requires upfront setup, while nginx remains a largely manual process dependent on external tools.

